+# The Daily Grind
+
+A static homepage with a progressively enhanced SMS sign-in form. No server or SMS provider is included. The form deliberately does not simulate sending messages or accept codes locally.
+
+## Authentication integration
+
+Serve over HTTPS and implement these same-origin JSON endpoints:
+
+- `POST /api/auth/request-code` receives `{ "phone": "+15551234567", "purpose": "rewards" }` (or `"order"`). Send an SMS and return `{ "challengeId": "opaque-server-issued-id" }` only after the provider accepts the request. Resends use the same endpoint and issue a fresh challenge.
+- `POST /api/auth/verify-code` receives `{ "challengeId": "opaque-server-issued-id", "code": "012345", "purpose": "rewards" }`. Validate the code on the server, establish an authenticated session with a secure HttpOnly cookie, and return `{ "verified": true }`. The client then navigates to `/rewards` or `/order-online`.
+
+Return JSON errors with appropriate HTTP status codes: 400/401 for invalid data or code, 410 for an expired challenge, and 429 for rate limiting. Apply server-side phone/code validation, short expiration, attempt limits, single-use codes, SMS request limits, and same-origin/CSRF protection. The client resend countdown is only a convenience, not rate-limit enforcement. This version accepts US mobile numbers.
+
+SMS messages must end with an origin-bound line using the actual hostname (no scheme, port, or path):
+
+```text
+Your Daily Grind verification code is 012345.
+
+@your-deployed-hostname.com #012345
+```
+
+WebOTP is feature-detected and its listener is aborted on submission, resend, number changes, timeout, and page exit. Browsers without it retain `autocomplete="one-time-code"` and manual entry. Failed API requests never advance the sign-in flow or claim the customer is authenticated.
+
+## Checks
+
+`node --check verification.js` checks JavaScript syntax. There is no build step. No development or preview server is needed for these checks.
